<robot name="work_cell">
  <!--
    The whole robot: a UR5e standing on the table, a Robotiq FT 300-S force
    sensor and a Robotiq 2F-85 gripper on its flange, and an Intel RealSense
    D405 depth camera beside the gripper.

    Every part of it is a real product whose maker publishes its own robot
    description, and this file's job is only to stack them and wire them to
    ros2_control. `ur_description` supplies the UR5e's links, joint limits and
    calibrated link lengths; `robotiq_description` supplies the gripper and
    the coupling that bolts it to a UR flange; `realsense2_description`
    supplies the camera. Nothing about their shapes is written here.

    See arm/gripper.urdf.xacro for why this particular gripper and this
    particular sensor, and arm/robotiq_2f85.py for the one thing that follows
    from using a real gripper: it is driven by a single knuckle angle rather
    than by a finger width.
  -->
  <!--
    Base UR robot series xacro macro.

    NOTE this is NOT a URDF. It cannot directly be loaded by consumers
    expecting a flattened '.urdf' file. See the top-level '.xacro' for that
    (but note that .xacro must still be processed by the xacro command).

    This file models the base kinematic chain of a UR robot, which then gets
    parameterised by various configuration files to convert it into a UR3(e),
    UR5(e), UR10(e) or UR16e, etc.

    NOTE the default kinematic parameters (i.e., link lengths, frame locations,
    offsets, etc) do not correspond to any particular robot. They are defaults
    only. There WILL be non-zero offsets between the Forward Kinematics results
    in TF (i.e., robot_state_publisher) and the values reported by the Teach
    Pendant.

    For accurate (and robot-specific) transforms, the 'kinematics_parameters_file'
    parameter MUST point to a .yaml file containing the appropriate values for
    the targeted robot.

    If using the UniversalRobots/Universal_Robots_ROS_Driver, follow the steps
    described in the readme of that repository to extract the kinematic
    calibration from the controller and generate the required .yaml file.

    Main author of the migration to yaml configs Ludovic Delval.

    Contributors to previous versions (in no particular order)

     - Denis Stogl
     - Lovro Ivanov
     - Felix Messmer
     - Kelsey Hawkins
     - Wim Meeussen
     - Shaun Edwards
     - Nadia Hammoudeh Garcia
     - Dave Hershberger
     - G. vd. Hoorn
     - Philip Long
     - Dave Coleman
     - Miguel Prada
     - Mathias Luedtke
     - Marcel Schnirring
     - Felix von Drigalski
     - Felix Exner
     - Jimmy Da Silva
     - Ajit Krisshna N L
     - Muhammad Asif Rana
  -->
  <!--
    The tool on the end of the arm: a Robotiq FT 300-S force/torque sensor on
    the flange, Robotiq's own UR coupling on the sensor, and a Robotiq 2F-85
    two-finger gripper on the coupling.

    Almost nothing about the gripper is described here, because Robotiq publish
    it. `robotiq_description` ships the links, the joint limits, the masses and
    the meshes for the 2F-85, and the coupling that bolts it to a UR flange, so
    this file includes that package and adds only what it does not cover: the
    force sensor, the friction of a silicone pad on glass, and a note about how
    the fingers are set up.

    Three things about this tool exist because it handles glass.

    **The fingers are pinned parallel.** The 2F-85 is an *adaptive* gripper:
    each finger has two joints driven by one motor, so left alone the fingers
    curl inwards and wrap around whatever they hold. That is useful for odd
    shapes and wrong for a glass, because this project's grip rules all assume
    two flat pads pressing on an upright piece of wall. Robotiq sell a parallel
    locking kit that pins the linkage so the pads stay parallel through the
    whole stroke, and the description below is of a gripper with that kit
    fitted — the fingertip joints mimic the knuckle at -1, which is exactly
    what keeps the pad faces level.

    **A force sensor sits between the flange and the gripper.** It is how the
    arm finds out what a glass really weighs, which no camera can tell it, and
    how it knows the rack has taken the weight before the fingers open. The
    UR5e has a force sensor built into its own flange, which would have been
    free, but Universal Robots publish +/-4.0 N of accuracy for it and a wine
    glass weighs about 2 N, so it cannot do this job. The FT 300-S resolves
    about 0.1 N.

    **The pads are silicone, so the friction is set here.** A rigid pad touches
    a curved glass wall at one point and all of the grip force goes through
    that point. A soft pad spreads over a small patch instead, which both holds
    better and is what makes a slightly sloping wall safe to grip: the pad
    conforms to the slope rather than sliding off it. The 6 degree figure in
    glasses/profile.py comes from how much slope a pad can take up. Robotiq's
    description says nothing about friction, because friction is not a property
    of a shape, so the surface below is this project's own.

    One thing worth knowing before reading the numbers elsewhere. The 2F-85 has
    **one driven joint**, a knuckle, and both fingers hang off it through a
    four-bar linkage. So the gripper cannot be told a width at all; it is told
    an angle, and the width follows. arm/robotiq_2f85.py does that translation
    and explains what else follows from it.

    Along the tool's z axis:
      0.0000  tool0, the flange
      0.0375  the force sensor ends, the coupling begins
      0.0485  the gripper's own base, where its description starts measuring
      0.1788  the middle of the pads with the gripper open (GRASP_DEPTH)
      0.1979  the fingertips (FINGERTIP_OFFSET)
  -->
  <!--
    An Intel RealSense D405 depth camera bolted beside the gripper, looking the
    same way the fingers point.

    The shape, the mass and the frames are Intel's own: `realsense2_description`
    ships the model, so this file includes it and adds only the mount and the
    Gazebo sensor that renders through it.

    The D405 is the short-range camera of the RealSense family, and that is why
    it is the one here. Its working range is about 70 mm to 500 mm, which is
    uncomfortably close for a mobile robot and exactly right for a camera that
    rides on a wrist and is stood 300 mm off a glass to measure it. The other
    common choice, the D435, starts at about 300 mm, so the closest look this
    task takes would be at the very edge of what it can do.

    Because it rides on the wrist, pointing the tool at something is the same
    as pointing the camera at it, and the arm can walk the camera around a
    glass to see whether it has a handle, which one side-on view cannot show.

    Two things about the real camera matter to the code that reads it.

    **Its depth and colour pictures come from the same place.** Intel's own
    description puts the colour sensor at no offset from the depth sensor on
    this model, unlike the D435, so one optical frame serves for both and
    glasses/perception.py can project a colour pixel and a depth pixel with the
    same arithmetic.

    **It sees much more than the camera this project used to assume.** The D405
    covers about 87 degrees across, where the invented camera covered 60. That
    changes how much table one picture holds and therefore how many places the
    arm has to stand to survey it. Nothing in the code needed changing for it,
    because the measurements read the focal length out of the camera's own
    `camera_info` message rather than assuming one.
  -->
  <material name="aluminum">
    <color rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>
  </material>
  <material name="plastic">
    <color rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1"/>
  </material>
  <!-- A link called "world" is how the simulator is told the base is bolted
       down rather than free to fall over. -->
  <link name="world"/>
  <!-- links -  main serial chain -->
  <!-- base_link serves as the root of the robot's kinematic tree. It follows REP-103
        conventions (i.e., X+ forward, Y+ left, Z+ up).
        Since some kinematic solvers forbid having inertia values attached to a root link, we've
        added the base_link_inertia link that carries the visual, collision and inertia
        information.
    -->
